      "parameters": {
        "description": "Generates a tailored cover letter for a job the user has already analyzed\nvia analyze_job, and saves it to the Applications sheet.\n\nCall this tool when the user asks you to write/generate a cover letter.\n\n- If the user does not specify which job, leave both job_title and company\n  empty — the tool will use the most recently analyzed job.\n- If the user names a company or job title (e.g. \"the Google one\",\n  \"the backend engineer role\"), pass whatever they said in job_title\n  and/or company — partial names are fine, matching is not exact.\n\nThe tool enforces the match-score gate itself: it will only generate a\nletter if that job's recommendation was \"Strong Match\" or \"Partial Match\".\nIf it was \"Weak Match\", the tool will NOT generate a letter — do not try\nto override this or generate the letter yourself.\n\nPossible outcomes you must handle:\n- status \"generated\": a letter was written and saved. Tell the user it's\n  ready and show them the cover_letter_text.\n- status \"skipped\": the job's match was too weak. Tell the user why,\n  using the reason field. Do not apologize excessively or offer to\n  generate one anyway.\n- status \"not_found\": no matching job was found in Applications. Ask the\n  user to double check the company/title, or to analyze the job first.\n- status \"needs_clarification\": more than one job matched. Read the\n  candidates list back to the user (job title, company, and date for\n  each) and ask which one they mean. Do not guess or pick one yourself.\n  Wait for their reply, then call this tool again with a more specific\n  job_title/company.",
        "workflowId": {
          "__rl": true,
          "value": "3mOsiTUTBKi1whd8",
          "mode": "list",
          "cachedResultUrl": "/workflow/3mOsiTUTBKi1whd8",
